<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Nullroute — runtime permissions pre-granted on first boot.
    Installs to /system_ext/etc/default-permissions/default-permissions-nullroute.xml

    Parsed by DefaultPermissionGrantPolicy. The <exception> element normally
    carries a sha256-cert-digest; it is omitted here because the package is
    platform-signed and preinstalled on the read-only system_ext partition, so
    the digest would only restate the image's own signing key.

    POST_NOTIFICATIONS is the only entry, and it is the one that matters most:
    two of the three health signals are user-visible only as notifications.

      * The "Not filtering" state (both .invalid probes fail) raises a
        persistent notification. If the user never granted notifications, a dead
        resolver hook looks exactly like a working one — which is the single
        failure mode this whole project is built to make loud (SPEC.md §10.2).
      * The breakage notifier ("Maps just failed a lookup for maps.gstatic.com —
        Allow?") is the recovery path for a false positive. Without it the user's
        only signal is an app that mysteriously does not work.

    fixed="false" so the user keeps full control and can revoke it in Settings
    like any other app. Pre-granting is about not losing the signal on a fresh
    flash, not about denying the user a choice.

    Nothing else is pre-granted. In particular there is no location, contacts,
    storage or phone-state grant here, because Nullroute requests none of them.
-->
<exceptions>
    <exception package="com.bestrom.nullroute">
        <permission name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" fixed="false"/>
    </exception>
</exceptions>
